{"outcome":"DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL","best_model_id":"PGS001795","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"Direct-match candidates for the target concept (uterine cancer) are present, including (i) exact/near-exact uterine cancer labels (PGS001795: “Uterine cancer”; PGS000541/PGS000542: “Malignant neoplasm of uterus”; PGS003740: “Corpus Uteri”) and (ii) the clinically dominant subtype (endometrial carcinoma/cancer: PGS000075, PGS000786, PGS002300, PGS002735-7, PGS003381, PGS004244), which domain knowledge states is a strong direct match for uterine carcinoma.

PGS001795 is preferred because its phenotyping_reported is explicitly “Uterine cancer” (highest endpoint fidelity to the target) and it uses a methodologically strong genome-wide shrinkage approach (PRS-CS-auto) with a very large variant set (911,692), with evaluation in EUR (n=170,276). Competing exact-label uterus scores (PGS000541/PGS000542, PGS003740) are sparse GWAS-hits/P+T/GWS SNP sets with weaker reported discrimination (e.g., AUROC ~0.57 for PGS000541/542) and/or small validation (PGS003740 n=372).

Main limitation: the available performance evidence for PGS001795 is not clearly strong on PRS-comparable metrics (Nagelkerke’s R2 covariates-regressed-out = 0.001948; AUROC shown is a full-model metric without a PRS-only AUROC), and endometrial-carcinoma models show higher full-model AUROC (e.g., PGS003381 AUROC 0.761) but are not exact-label uterine cancer and also largely lack PRS-only metrics. Given these comparability limits and reliance on full-model metrics across the set, the direct-match recommendation is supportive but not high-certainty."}

{"outcome":"DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL","best_model_id":"PGS000471","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"All three candidates (PGS000469/470/471) are direct matches: trait_reported, trait_efo, and phenotyping_reported consistently specify “Carcinoma in situ of skin,” aligning with the target “skin carcinoma in situ.” They are also closely comparable in design (same publication family, EUR-only GWAS/DEV/EVAL, same training cohort MGI, same basic covariates age/sex/batch/PCs, same validation sample size n=5,500), so selection can lean on the within-family performance package.

Among the three, PGS000471 (lassosum) shows the strongest visible validation performance in the provided full-model metrics and effect sizes: highest AUROC (0.569 vs 0.557 vs 0.524), highest Nagelkerke R² (0.0255 vs 0.0154 vs 0.00141), highest OR top 1% vs rest (3.77 vs 2.45 vs 1.48), and highest per-SD OR (1.401 vs 1.308 vs 1.09). 

Main limitation: none of the candidates reports PRS-only (covariate-free) AUROC/R², so the reported AUROCs are full-model metrics and not strictly comparable as standalone PRS performance; this keeps confidence below high despite the consistent endpoint/covariate context across siblings."}
